.TH SANDBOX.YAML 5 "2026-07-03" "sudo-logger" "Sudo-logger Configuration"
.SH NAME
sandbox.yaml \- Configuration file for the sudo-logger eBPF LSM process sandbox
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sandbox.yaml
defines a deny-list of files, devices, and processes that are protected against
modification or interference by processes running inside a \fBsudo-logger\fR session.

The restrictions are enforced at the kernel level using \fBeBPF LSM\fR hooks,
making them effective even against the root user within a sandboxed session.
20 hooks (18 LSM + 2 tracepoints) are attached in total:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsm/file_open\fR, \fBlsm/file_permission\fR: Deny write-mode opens on protected inodes.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsm/path_truncate\fR, \fBlsm/inode_setattr\fR: Prevent truncation attacks.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsm/inode_unlink\fR, \fBlsm/inode_rename\fR: Deny deletion or renaming of protected files.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsm/inode_create\fR, \fBlsm/inode_mkdir\fR, \fBlsm/inode_mknod\fR, \fBlsm/inode_symlink\fR: Prevent file creation inside protected directories.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsm/task_kill\fR: Prevent sending signals to protected processes.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsm/socket_create\fR: Prevent creating \fBAF_NETLINK\fR sockets (firewall/route tampering).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsm/bprm_check_security\fR: Block execution of binaries in \fBforbidden\fR or under \fBnoexec\fR directories (trusted package managers exempt).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsm/ptrace_access_check\fR: Prevent attaching to/debugging processes outside the sandbox.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsm/sb_mount\fR: Prevent mounting a filesystem over a protected inode.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsm/capable\fR: Restrict specific capabilities (\fBCAP_NET_ADMIN\fR, \fBCAP_AUDIT_CONTROL\fR, \fBCAP_SYS_MODULE\fR, \fBCAP_MAC_ADMIN\fR, \fBCAP_SYS_RAWIO\fR, \fBCAP_SYS_BOOT\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsm/unix_stream_connect\fR: Block D-Bus/systemd control-socket connections when \fBdeny_systemd_ipc\fR is enabled.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsm/bpf\fR: Prevent tampering with the sandbox's own BPF maps.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtp_btf/sched_process_fork\fR: Propagate sandbox state to all child processes at fork time.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtp_btf/sched_process_exit\fR: Remove a process from sandbox tracking on exit.
.SH FILE FORMAT
The file uses YAML format. This page documents the \fBprotect\fR key (the
deny-list) and \fBtrusted_package_managers\fR (narrow exemptions from it);
see the project's YAML reference documentation for the top-level
\fBenabled\fR and \fBfeatures\fR keys.
.PP
.RS
.nf
\fBprotect:\fR
  \fBfiles:\fR
    \- /etc/sudoers
    \- /etc/sudo-logger
  \fBforbidden:\fR
    \- /usr/bin/firewall\-cmd
  \fBnoexec:\fR
    \- /tmp
    \- /dev/shm
  \fBdevices:\fR
    \- /dev/nvme0n1
  \fBproc:\fR
    \- /proc/sysrq-trigger
  \fBsockets:\fR
    \- /run/sudo-logger/plugin.sock
  \fBprocesses:\fR
    \- auditd
    \- sshd
\fBtrusted_package_managers:\fR
  \fBenabled:\fR true
  \fBbinaries:\fR
    \- /usr/bin/dnf5
    \- /usr/bin/rpm
  \fBallow_create_in:\fR
    \- /usr/lib/systemd/system
    \- /etc/systemd/system
    \- /usr/lib/systemd/system-preset
    \- /etc/pki/rpm-gpg
.fi
.RE
.SH SECTIONS
.TP
.B files
A list of absolute paths to regular files or directories.
Protected files cannot be opened for writing, truncated, deleted, or renamed.
Protected directories cannot have new files or subdirectories created inside
them. Both restrictions can be narrowly relaxed for \fBdnf\fR/\fBrpm\fR via
\fBtrusted_package_managers\fR below; in-place writes and truncation of an
existing protected file are never relaxed.
Protection is recursive for all existing files found inside a directory at agent
startup, to a depth of 3. Symlinks are protected by their own inode and are not
followed — a symlink to another directory does not bring that directory's tree
into the protected set.
.TP
.B forbidden
A list of absolute paths to binaries forbidden from execution inside any
session, matched by inode (renaming the binary is still blocked; copying it
to a new path creates a new inode that is not covered — pair with
\fBnoexec\fR to close that gap). A process carrying the
\fBtrusted_package_managers\fR marker (\fBdnf\fR/\fBrpm\fR and their forked
scriptlets) is exempt and only alerted \(em package triggers legitimately
exec \fBsystemd-run\fR (systemd's \fB%filetrigger\fR does
\fBsystemctl --user -M\fR, whose \fBsd-bus\fR machine transport is
\fBunixexec:path=systemd-run\fR).
.TP
.B noexec
A list of directories under which execution is blocked entirely, preventing
a forbidden binary from being copied elsewhere and run from there. As with
\fBforbidden\fR, a \fBtrusted_package_managers\fR process is exempt and only
alerted.
\fBWARNING:\fR not safe on Btrfs — every subvolume root (e.g. \fB/\fR,
\fB/home\fR) shares inode 256 on the same device, so listing a subvolume
root also matches \fB/\fR and blocks all execution in the session. Safe on
ext4 and xfs only; the agent logs a warning if inode 256 is detected.
.PP
\fBNote:\fR listing \fB/var/tmp\fR also affects RPM, which stages and executes every
package scriptlet there by default (\fB%_tmppath\fR). \fBsudo\-logger\-client\fR
1.20.126+ (v1.39.3+) ships an RPM macro redirecting scriptlet execution to a
dedicated, non\-noexec directory so this does not collide; on an older client, set
\fB%_tmppath\fR manually rather than removing \fB/var/tmp\fR from this list. See
\fBsudo-logger-agent\fR(8) and the troubleshooting guide for details.
.TP
.B devices
A list of device nodes (e.g., \fB/dev/sda\fR). Prevents raw hardware access.
.TP
.B proc
A list of entries in the \fB/proc\fR filesystem. Prevents modification of sensitive kernel parameters.
.TP
.B sockets
A list of Unix domain socket files. Prevents deletion or replacement of the socket file.
Does \fBnot\fR block \fBconnect(2)\fR or data transmission to the socket.
.TP
.B processes
A list of process names (\fBcomm\fR). Processes in the sandbox are forbidden
from sending \fBany\fR signal (including \fBSIGKILL\fR and \fBSIGTERM\fR)
to these processes.
.IP
This effectively prevents a sandboxed root user from stopping protected systemd
services using \fBsystemctl stop\fR or \fBkill\fR.
.IP
\fBNOTE:\fR Linux process names are limited to 15 characters. Longer names are
truncated. Use \fBcat /proc/<pid>/comm\fR to verify the exact name.
.SH TRUSTED_PACKAGE_MANAGERS
Off by default when the key is absent; the shipped example
\fB/etc/sudo-logger/sandbox.yaml\fR enables it. Grants a narrow, explicit
exemption to a small set of trusted binaries so that \fBdnf\fR/\fBrpm\fR can
complete real package transactions while running inside a monitored \fBsudo\fR
session, instead of requiring updates to run outside \fBsudo\fR entirely.
Within an \fBallow_create_in\fR directory the exemption covers creating new
files/dirs/symlinks, renaming a staging file over an existing one (how
\fBrpm\fR installs every payload file \(em it never writes in place), and
deleting a file. In-place writes and truncation are never exempted, and
nothing outside \fBallow_create_in\fR is affected. Independently of
\fBallow_create_in\fR, the marked process (and its forked scriptlet subtree)
is also exempt from the \fBforbidden\fR and \fBnoexec\fR execution blocks so
that package triggers can run \fBsystemd-run\fR etc. Signal
(\fBprocesses\fR), capability, \fBptrace\fR, mount and netlink enforcement
are never lifted. Every exempted operation is alerted (\fBallowed=true\fR).
.TP
.B enabled
Master switch. \fBfalse\fR (or absent) leaves directory protection fully
enforced with no exemption, identical to omitting this key.
.TP
.B binaries
Exec targets, matched by inode at exec time exactly like \fBforbidden\fR \(em
not by path string, so copying a different binary to a trusted path does not
gain the exemption. Trust propagates to forked descendants (an RPM scriptlet's
\fB/bin/sh\fR needs the same exemption its parent had) and is never revoked
mid-tree.
.TP
.B allow_create_in
Directories where the binaries above (and their descendants) may create new
files, directories, and symlinks, rename a staging file over an existing
entry, and delete an entry \(em even though the directory is also listed
under \fBfiles\fR. Deliberately narrower than \fBfiles\fR: a directory here
has no effect unless it is \fBalso\fR listed there; opening an existing
protected file for writing or truncating it is never exempted; and a rename
that would move a protected file \fIout\fR of an \fBallow_create_in\fR
directory, or into one that is only under \fBfiles\fR, is still blocked.
.PP
Every exempted operation still generates a full sandbox alert
(\fBallowed=true\fR instead of a block) rather than becoming silent; the
default \fBrisk-rules.yaml\fR surfaces this via the
\fBsandbox_trusted_pkgmgr_write\fR rule in the replay-server session list.
.PP
\fBLimitation:\fR the exemption only reaches directories in
\fBallow_create_in\fR. \fBsudo-logger-client\fR's own upgrade still fails via
\fBsudo\fR because its agent and plugin binaries
(\fI/usr/bin/sudo-logger-agent\fR, \fI/usr/libexec/sudo/sudo_logger_plugin.so\fR)
are under \fBfiles\fR but deliberately \fInot\fR under \fBallow_create_in\fR \(em
letting a package replace them in place is exactly the tamper this guards
against (\fBSANDBOX VIOLATION action=FILE_RENAME\fR). Install client upgrades
as direct root instead (\fBsu -\fR, then \fBdnf\fR/\fBrpm\fR directly), which
bypasses the sandbox entirely since it only hooks \fBsudo\fR-mediated
sessions, or temporarily disable the sandbox via the replay-server UI for the
upgrade window. A \fBsudo dnf update\fR of ordinary packages that ship files
under \fI/usr/lib/systemd/system\fR and the other \fBallow_create_in\fR
directories now completes normally.
.SH SCOPE
Restrictions only apply to processes that are:
.IP 1.
Members of a \fBsudo-logger\fR session cgroup.
.IP 2.
Descendants of a \fBsudo\fR process tracked by the agent since session start.
.PP
Processes outside of active \fBsudo-logger\fR sessions are completely unaffected.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Requires \fBCONFIG_BPF_LSM=y\fR and \fBbpf\fR present in the active LSM list
(verify with \fBgrep bpf /sys/kernel/security/lsm\fR).
On Fedora\ 38 and later \fBbpf\fR is active by default; no boot parameter
changes are needed.
On older or non-Fedora kernels add \fBlsm=bpf\fR to \fBGRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX\fR
and run \fBgrub2-mkconfig\fR.
.SH FILES
.I /etc/sudo-logger/sandbox.yaml
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sudo-logger-agent (8),
.BR sudo_logger_plugin (8)
